<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="indole-3-acetate inactivation VIII" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6219" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Indole-3-acetic acid (IAA), a prevalent form of auxin, is an important phytohormone that affects many aspects of plant development throughout the whole plant life cycle. IAA also plays a crucial role in allowing plants to respond to the biotic and abiotic stimuli they encounter |CITS: [15749753]|. IAA is subject to a number of biochemical modifications that regulate its activity and allow plants to fine tune their levels of active auxin |CITS: [15749753]|. IAA can be conjugated to a number of amino acids through the activity of enzymes with structural similarity to the firefly luciferase family of adenylate forming enzymes |CITS: [15659623]|. Some of these amino acid modications can be reversed through the activity of amido hydrolases |CITS:[7792259][10072397]|, suggesting that some compounds, such as IAA-alanine and IAA-leucine, are storage forms of auxin that can be metabolized to contribute to the pool of free auxin |CITS: [15659623]|. Other IAA-amino acid conjugates, such as IAA-aspartate, are targeted for further breakdown (|CITS:[9733548]| (see |FRAME:PWY-2021|) and act as intermediates in IAA catabolic pathways. Given the importance of auxin homeostatic maintenance, the transcripts encoding several IAA-amido synthetases are up-regulated in response to applications of free auxin (rev in |CITS:[15659623]|). Together with an array of other IAA-based modification pathways (see |FRAME:PWY-1782|), IAA-amino acid conjugation appears to play a vital role in regulating the active levels of a potent phytohormone.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE" />
    <Author name="dreher" />
  </Authors>
